{0 Pipeline Cost and Sharing Model}

This note fixes the cost model that should guide proof-generation work before
new backend optimizations are added. The goal is to make proof failures and
timeouts explainable from the recovered automata down to Why3 and SMT tasks.

The important discipline is semantic: cost reductions may share, factor, or
name information, but they must not remove product cases unless a separate
certified reachability argument justifies their use. In particular, this note
must be read together with {!page-temporal_phase_semantics} and
{!page-product_proof_pipeline_audit}.

{1 Current diagnosis}

The medical infusion example exposed a pipeline cost problem before exposing a
useful SMT counterexample. The reduced examples validate, while the full
example spends its time and memory in Why3 declaration typing and VC
construction.

The current measurements show three distinct effects:

- grouping safe postconditions by product step drastically reduces the number
  of generated helper functions;
- disabling formula sharing makes the WhyML artifact explode, so inlining is
  not viable for the full example;
- formula sharing reduces the artifact size, but large shared predicate
  declarations can still be expensive for Why3 to open and type.

The next changes therefore need a whole-pipeline cost report. A backend-only
threshold is too local: it can hide the point where duplication was introduced
or where a fact lost its useful structure.

{1 Cost stages}

{2 Source and elaboration}

The source language introduces program state, inputs, observers, assumptions,
and guarantees. At this point formulas still have useful origin information.

The cost report should record:

- number of public assumptions and guarantees;
- number of observer definitions and their free variables;
- formula size, temporal depth, and operator distribution;
- input, output, and state variables mentioned by each specification item;
- source locations for large formulas.

This is where we should detect a specification whose public guarantees do not
use the assumptions or observers that were introduced for them.

{2 Specification automata}

The LTL layer turns assumptions and guarantees into automata. This can multiply
formula occurrences before the product is even built.

The cost report should record, separately for assumption and guarantee
automata:

- number of states and edges;
- number of accepting, rejecting, and bad states;
- edge guard size and guard fanout;
- temporal-depth distribution before and after automaton construction;
- repeated guard subformulas and their origin formulas.

The important invariant is that automaton edges are semantic cases. They may be
named and grouped later, but not silently removed for cost reasons.

{2 Product construction}

The product combines program transitions, assumption edges, and guarantee
edges. Its raw cost is driven by the fanout of these three components.

For each source product state, the cost report should record:

- outgoing program transitions;
- outgoing assumption edges;
- outgoing guarantee edges;
- raw product-step candidates;
- classified safe, bad-assumption, and bad-guarantee cases;
- grouping keys used by the generator;
- maximum and total guard size per group.

No pruning should happen here because a mixed formula looks inconsistent.
Mixed formulas often combine facts evaluated in different phases of the
instant. Any later exclusion of a product source must be justified through the
certified reachability invariant described in
{!page-temporal_phase_semantics}.

{2 Step summaries}

Product-step summaries are the first place where proof cost becomes visible:
safe cases are grouped, bad cases are kept, and propagation requirements are
computed.

The cost report should record:

- number of [product_step_summary] values;
- number of safe and unsafe cases per summary;
- size of the safe-case disjunction;
- size and origin of [local_requires];
- size and origin of [propagation_requires];
- whether a summary uses a certified reachability fact [R_p].

This stage must also preserve phase information. Program guards and
assumption-edge guards belong to the step context. They must not be shifted
into the next instant as previous-tick facts.

{2 Temporal lowering}

The lowering of [pre], phase formulas, and observer histories can duplicate
the same mathematical fact under several syntactic forms.

The cost report should record:

- maximum [pre] depth;
- number of generated history variables;
- expansion factor from source formulas to lowered formulas;
- formula identifiers before and after lowering;
- phase of every lowered fact: previous tick, current tick, or step context.

The expected output is not just a larger formula. It should be a formula DAG
with stable identifiers, so later passes can distinguish genuine duplication
from necessary phase-specific copies.

{2 Proof kernel}

The proof kernel turns summaries into clauses and obligations. This is the
right place to remove tautological clauses and to count semantic duplication.

The cost report should record:

- number of clauses by origin;
- number of hypotheses and conclusions per clause;
- tautological clauses removed;
- formulas with high fanout across clauses;
- formulas that appear in both entry and post clauses;
- clauses protected by [R_p] reachability facts.

Removing [true] clauses is a representation cleanup. Removing a product case
is not. The report should make this distinction explicit.

{2 Backend planning}

Before emitting WhyML, the backend should make an explicit plan for grouping
and sharing. The plan should be reportable independently of the generated text.

The cost report should record:

- number of helper functions;
- number of helpers per product source;
- number of [requires] and [ensures] before and after grouping;
- candidate shared formulas with size, fanout, arity, and origin;
- selected global predicates;
- selected local cuts;
- formulas intentionally left inline.

The backend should choose among four representations:

- inline a formula when it is small or used once;
- define a global predicate when a large formula has high fanout and moderate
  arity;
- introduce a local cut when a large formula is reused inside one helper but
  not globally;
- use a certified product-state fact [R_p] when the fact is about
  end-of-instant reachability.

Each representation has a different cost. Global predicates reduce text size
but can increase Why3 declaration-typing work, especially when they have large
arity or depend on many other predicates. Local cuts preserve VC locality but
may duplicate proof effort. [R_p] facts can remove spurious source obligations
only if their preservation obligations have already been generated.

{2 Why3 declaration and VC generation}

The Why3 frontend has its own costs before any SMT solver runs. Large predicate
definitions, large contracts, and repeated opening of definitions can dominate
the execution.

The cost report should record:

- WhyML line count and byte size;
- number of logic declarations;
- number of predicate definitions;
- maximum predicate arity;
- dependency depth among shared predicates;
- number of generated VCs;
- time spent before the first SMT task is sent.

If the proof process stops here, the problem is not yet a failed proof. It is a
backend representation problem.

{2 SMT tasks}

Only after Why3 has produced tasks do solver-level costs become meaningful.

The cost report should record:

- number of tasks by obligation origin;
- task size distribution;
- solver time distribution;
- valid, invalid, timeout, unknown, and resource-limit results;
- largest formulas still expanded in solver tasks.

This is the stage where adding lemmas or cuts may be justified by a concrete
solver bottleneck. It should not be used to guess at problems that actually
occur earlier in Why3 declaration typing.

{1 Fact representation}

The central object should be a fact, not only a formula string. A fact carries
enough metadata to make sharing and phase discipline checkable:

{[
type fact = {
  id : fact_id;
  formula : hexpr;
  origin : fact_origin;
  phase : PreviousTick | CurrentTick | StepTickContext;
  scope : fact_scope;
  dependencies : fact_id list;
  free_variables : variable list;
  size : int;
  temporal_depth : int;
  structural_hash : string;
  fanout : int;
}
]}

The sharing decision should consume this record. It should not rediscover
sharing from the final WhyML text, where phase, origin, and product-state
meaning are already mostly lost.

{1 Sound sharing}

The following transformations are sound representation changes:

- replacing repeated formulas by definitional predicates;
- grouping conclusions that are already under the same source precondition;
- introducing a local cut together with the corresponding proof obligation;
- using [R_p] as a previous-tick hypothesis only when its initialization and
  preservation obligations are generated.

The following transformations are not mere cost optimizations:

- deleting product edges;
- filtering mixed-phase guards by syntactic contradiction;
- shifting program or assumption guards into later previous-tick hypotheses;
- assuming a shared predicate without defining it or proving the associated
  cut;
- hiding an unproved reachability fact in the backend.

These boundaries are required for correction, progression, and relative
completeness with respect to the Rocq formalization.

{1 Rocq alignment}

The Rocq development should not need to know Why3-specific thresholds, but it
must see the same semantic objects:

- product-step enumeration;
- grouped disjunctions for safe successors;
- proof-kernel clauses;
- certified reachability facts [R_p];
- definitional sharing or explicit cuts.

Thresholds such as "share formulas larger than 120 nodes" are backend
heuristics. They must elaborate to either a definitional abbreviation or an
explicit cut in the common proof view. The formal argument should be invariant
under changing those thresholds.

{1 Proposed instrumentation}

The next implementation step should be metrics-only:

{[
kairos ... --dump-cost-report path/to/report.json
]}

The report should contain one section per stage:

{[
{
  "source": { ... },
  "automata": { ... },
  "product": { ... },
  "summaries": { ... },
  "temporal_lowering": { ... },
  "proof_kernel": { ... },
  "backend_plan": { ... },
  "why3": { ... },
  "smt": { ... }
}
]}

A compact text rendering can be added for humans, but JSON should be the
reference format so benchmark scripts can compare runs.

The first useful report for the medical example should answer these questions:

- which stage introduces the largest formula expansion;
- which product sources dominate the number of obligations;
- which facts have the highest fanout;
- which shared predicates have high arity or deep dependency chains;
- whether the process reaches SMT tasks or stops in Why3 declaration typing.

Only after this report exists should we tune sharing thresholds or introduce
new local cuts.
